---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="VPS deployment (bare metal)"
  path="/docs/self-hosting/vps"
  description="Deploy KoAkademy on a plain Linux VPS with Docker Compose and Caddy for TLS."
>
  <p>
    When you want full control without a PaaS: a plain Linux VPS — Hetzner,
    DigitalOcean, Vultr, Oracle Cloud, or any other — running the Compose stack
    behind Caddy. This guide covers the surrounding server work that the
    platform guides assume is already done. Container deployment is the
    supported production path; the prebuilt image is the runtime KoAkademy
    ships and tests.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ubuntu 24.04 LTS or Debian 12 VPS with at least 4 GB RAM for an evaluation install; size from observed workload</li>
    <li>A domain pointing to the server's public IP</li>
    <li>Docker Engine with the Compose plugin</li>
    <li>An external S3-compatible bucket for uploads</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Harden the server</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Log in with SSH keys only; disable password authentication and root login over SSH.</li>
    <li>Create a non-root user with <code>sudo</code> and run the deployment steps as that user.</li>
    <li>Open the firewall for <code>22</code>, <code>80</code>, and <code>443</code> only. Port <code>8000</code> stays loopback-only behind the proxy.</li>
    <li>Enable <code>fail2ban</code>, automatic security updates, and a swap file if the workload needs headroom.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
sudo ufw allow 443/tcp
sudo ufw enable</code></pre>

  <h2>2. Install Docker Engine</h2>
  <p>
    Install Docker Engine and the Compose plugin from Docker's official
    repository for your distribution, then verify:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker --version
docker compose version</code></pre>

  <h2>3. Obtain a release</h2>
  <p>
    KoAkademy supports only the latest stable release. Check out the matching
    tag and use its production files as the source of truth:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone --depth 1 --branch &lt;latest-stable-tag&gt; https://github.com/yukazakiri/koakademy.git
cd koakademy
cp .env.production.example .env
chmod 600 .env</code></pre>
  <p>
    Set every value in <code>.env</code>: <code>APP_URL</code> as the HTTPS
    origin, <code>PORTAL_HOST</code> and <code>ADMIN_HOST</code>,
    <code>DB_*</code>, <code>REDIS_*</code>, <code>GOTENBERG_URL</code>,
    <code>AWS_*</code> storage credentials, <code>MAIL_*</code>, and
    <code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>. See
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/configuration">Configuration</a> for every
    variable. Generate the application key:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml run --rm app php artisan key:generate --show</code></pre>
  <p>
    Copy the complete output into <code>APP_KEY</code>. Validate the stack
    before starting anything:
  </p>
  <pre><code>docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml config --quiet</code></pre>

  <h2>4. Launch the stack</h2>
  <p>Start the dependencies first, migrate as a deliberate step, then start the application:</p>
  <pre><code>docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml pull
docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml up -d postgres redis gotenberg
docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml run --rm app php artisan migrate --force
docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml up -d app
curl --fail --silent --show-error http://127.0.0.1:8000/up</code></pre>

  <h2>5. Caddy as the HTTPS edge</h2>
  <p>
    Install Caddy from its official repository, then configure the site. Caddy
    terminates TLS, obtains certificates automatically, and preserves the
    forwarded headers KoAkademy needs:
  </p>
  <pre><code>school.example &#123;
    encode zstd gzip
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    Restart Caddy and verify <code>https://school.example/up</code>. With the
    application bound to loopback only, <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> is
    appropriate here — the warning in
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/configuration">Configuration</a> applies when
    the application is reachable from any other host.
  </p>
  <p>
    For split portal/admin subdomains, add both hostnames to Caddy and to
    <code>PORTAL_HOST</code> and <code>ADMIN_HOST</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Complete setup</h2>
  <p>
    Visit <code>https://school.example/setup</code> to create the institution,
    initial academic period, and first administrator, then verify
    <code>/admin</code>. The setup route returns <code>403</code> once the
    application is initialized.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Backups</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Schedule a nightly <code>pg_dump</code> of the PostgreSQL database and
      push it offsite with <code>restic</code> or <code>rclone</code> to
      Backblaze B2, an S3 bucket, or a Hetzner Storage Box. Test a restore
      periodically.
    </li>
    <li>Back up the S3 upload bucket with the provider's versioning controls.</li>
    <li>Store <code>.env</code> and <code>APP_KEY</code> in an encrypted secret store, never in the repository.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Example nightly dump (adjust names and secrets):</p>
  <pre><code>docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml exec -T postgres \
  pg_dump --clean --if-exists --no-owner --username="$DB_USERNAME" "$DB_DATABASE" &gt; koakademy.sql</code></pre>
  <p>
    Run this from a shell where <code>DB_USERNAME</code> and
    <code>DB_DATABASE</code> are loaded (for example <code>set -a; source .env; set +a</code>),
    or substitute the configured non-secret database name and user directly.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Upgrades and rollback</h2>
  <p>
    Upgrades follow the manual Compose procedure: back up, update the checked-out
    tag and <code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>, pull the new image, run
    <code>migrate --force</code>, replace the application, and smoke-test. The
    complete rules — including when a code-only rollback is safe — are in
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/deployment">Deployment</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Monitoring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An external uptime check against <code>/up</code></li>
    <li><code>docker compose --env-file .env -f compose.production.yaml logs --tail=200 app</code> for application logs</li>
    <li>Disk usage, queue depth, certificate expiry, and backup success</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="doc-callout doc-callout-note">
    <p class="doc-callout-label">Why containers on a VPS</p>
    <p>
      The prebuilt KoAkademy image is the supported runtime — it bundles
      FrankenPHP, the required PHP extensions, and the process supervisor.
      Building and running KoAkademy from source on bare PHP is not covered
      here; if you need that control, use the image as the base and maintain
      your own build.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
